\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{Exact image densities and elimination towers}
\date{2026-11-11}

\p{Let #{\mathcal B:X_f\to X_c} be a
\vocabk{coarse path map}{fdip-00MO}, put
#{\rho=\mathcal B_*\nu_f}, and let #{w\ge0} be a Borel function
in #{L^1(\nu_f)}. Put #{Z=\int w\,\extd\nu_f}. There is a unique
nonnegative #{L^1(\rho)} class #{f} such that
##{\mathcal B_*(w\nu_f)=f\rho,\qquad
 \int_{X_c}f\,\extd\rho=Z.}
For every bounded Borel #{F:X_c\to\mathbb C},
##{\int F(\mathcal BU)w(U)\,\extd\nu_f(U)=\int F(u)f(u)\,\extd\rho(u).}
If #{Z>0}, the normalized coarse law is #{Z^{-1}f\rho}.}

\p{If #{G} is compact metrizable, there is a Borel probability
kernel #{\kappa(u,dU)} that disintegrates #{\nu_f} over
#{\mathcal B}. It is supported on #{\mathcal B^{-1}\{u\}}
for #{\rho}-almost every #{u}, and
##{f(u)=\int w(U)\,\kappa(u,dU)
       =\mathbb E_{\nu_f}[w\mid\mathcal B=u]
       \quad\text{for }\rho\text{-almost every }u.}
No regular conditional kernel is asserted for a general
compact Hausdorff group.}

\p{For any measurable #{C:X_c\to Y}, set #{\sigma=C_*\rho}.
If #{h} is the density of #{C_*(f\rho)} relative to #{\sigma},
then
##{(C\circ\mathcal B)_*(w\nu_f)=h\sigma,\qquad
 \int h\,\extd\sigma=Z.}
Equivalently, #{h\circ C} represents
#{\mathbb E_\rho[f\mid\sigma(C)]}, and
#{h\circ C\circ\mathcal B} represents
#{\mathbb E_{\nu_f}[w\mid\sigma(C\circ\mathcal B)]}.
These equalities retain weights that vanish or are unbounded;
they do not divide by #{f}.}

\p{If #{w} is gauge invariant as an #{L^1(\nu_f)} class,
then #{f\rho} is gauge invariant and, for each #{a\in K_c},
#{f(a^{-1}\cdot u)=f(u)} for #{\rho}-almost every #{u}.
When #{G} is compact metrizable, #{f} has a finite nonnegative
Borel representative invariant at every point.}

\proof{
\p{If #{\rho(A)=0}, then #{\nu_f(\mathcal B^{-1}A)=0}, so
#{\int_{\mathcal B^{-1}A}w\,\extd\nu_f=0}. Thus the two finite
measures satisfy
##{\mathcal B_*(w\nu_f)\ll\rho.}
The Radon–Nikodym theorem
\citet{Theorem 2.10, p. 29}{kallenberg2002foundations}
gives #{f}; positivity and evaluation on the whole space give
its sign and mass. Testing indicators, then simple functions
and bounded limits, gives the displayed observable identity.}

\p{In the metrizable case both finite configuration products
are compact metric spaces, hence standard Borel. Apply
\citet{Theorem 6.3, p. 107}{kallenberg2002foundations}
to the identity random element of #{X_f} and the random
element #{\mathcal B}. The conditional integration formula
of Theorem 6.4, p. 108, identifies the displayed kernel
integral with the density already obtained. Applying it to
the measurable indicator of #{\mathcal BU\ne u} shows that
#{\kappa(u,\mathcal B^{-1}\{u\})=1} almost everywhere.
This uses measurability of the diagonal in #{X_c\times X_c}.}

\p{For a measurable #{A\subseteq Y},
##{\int_Ah\,\extd\sigma
 =\int_{C^{-1}A}f\,\extd\rho
 =\int_{(C\circ\mathcal B)^{-1}A}w\,\extd\nu_f.}
These tests prove both the composed image identity and the
conditional-expectation assertions. They are the tower property
of \citet{Theorem 6.1(vii), p. 104}{kallenberg2002foundations}.
They also prove preservation of #{Z}, including #{Z=0}.
The algebraic [weighted elimination tower](fdip-00KX)
has additional unit and domain hypotheses; no such
invertibility is needed in this measure identity.}

\p{By \ref{fdip-00MP}, each #{a\in K_c} has a lift to #{K_f}.
Gauge invariance of #{w\nu_f} and equivariance show that its
image is invariant. Since #{\rho} is invariant as well,
uniqueness of the Radon–Nikodym density gives the claimed
#{L^1} equality for each #{a}.}

\p{For the everywhere invariant version in the metrizable
case, first choose a finite nonnegative Borel representative
#{f_0}; an infinite value on its null exceptional set can be
replaced by zero. The function
##{\bar f(u)=\int_{K_c}f_0(a^{-1}\cdot u)\,\extd\mu_{K_c}(a)}
is Borel with values in #{[0,\infty]}. Joint measurability and
Tonelli apply on these compact metrizable spaces. Haar
invariance makes #{\bar f} invariant at every point. Moreover,
##{\int_{K_c}\int_{X_c}
 |f_0(a^{-1}\cdot u)-f_0(u)|\,\extd\rho(u)\,\extd\mu_{K_c}(a)=0,}
because the inner integral vanishes for each #{a}. Hence
#{\bar f=f_0} almost everywhere. Its infinite-value set is
Borel, invariant and null; replacing #{\bar f} there by zero
gives the asserted finite invariant representative. This
step does not infer simultaneous pointwise invariance from
an uncountable family of separate almost-everywhere equalities.}
}
